.\" unexpand.1 -- maux's unexpand(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text), so it carries no
.\" third-party notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as
.\" cut.1/fold.1. Rendered at build time to build/man/UNEXPAND.TXT
.\" and staged on the play image as /usr/man/UNEXPAND:
.\" `more /usr/man/UNEXPAND`.
.TH UNEXPAND 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
unexpand \- convert spaces to tabs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unexpand
[
.B \-a
] [
.BI \-t " tablist"
] [
.I file
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Unexpand
copies each named
.I file
(standard input if none is given) to standard output, replacing
runs of two or more blanks that reach a tab stop with a tab.
.PP
Without
.B \-a
or
.BR \-t ,
only a
.I leading
run \(em one starting at the very first column of the line \(em
is a candidate; the instant a non\-blank byte is seen, the rest
of that line is copied through untouched.
.B \-a
converts a qualifying run anywhere in the line, not just a
leading one; a bare
.B \-t
implies
.BR \-a .
.PP
.I Tablist
is as for
.IR expand (1):
a single number sets periodic stops that many columns apart (8
by default); two or more increasing numbers set explicit stops,
past which no further run can qualify.
.PP
A run that crosses more than one stop collapses one tab per
stop crossed, not one tab for the whole run; any blanks left
over that never reach a stop stay literal.  A run of exactly one
blank is never converted, even sitting exactly on a stop \(em
there is no length saved.  A backspace counts as a member of the
current run (its own column effect is \-1, never below 0)
without itself ending the run.  The column resets at the start
of each operand.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/UNEXPAND  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
expand(1), fold(1), cut(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if a file named on the command line could
not be opened \(em that file is skipped, a diagnostic naming it
is written to standard error, and the remaining operands are
still processed; 2 for a usage error, including a malformed or
non\-increasing
.I tablist
or a stop over 120.
.SH BUGS
An explicit tab stop is capped at 120 columns, the same ceiling
as
.IR expand (1)'s
own.
